<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="brassinolide biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-2582" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: Brassinosteroids Brassinosteroids| are steroidal phytohormones that regulate various important physiological and developmental processes, such as cell elongation and division, pollen fertility, photomorphogenesis, differentiation of vascular elements, and stress resistance |CITS: [Mandava88][15012241]|. Brassinosteroids, which have structural similarity to |FRAME: ECDYSONE|, the molting hormone of insects, are C&lt;sub&gt;27&lt;/sub&gt;, C&lt;sub&gt;28&lt;/sub&gt; and C&lt;sub&gt;29&lt;/sub&gt; steroids depending on their C-24 alkyl substituents. More than 70 free, naturally occurring brassinosteroids have been identified in plants. The C&lt;sub&gt;28&lt;/sub&gt; brassinosteroid |FRAME: CPD-697| (BL) is the most biologically active and together with its C&lt;sub&gt;28&lt;/sub&gt; congeners is distributed widely in the plant kingdom |CITS:[15012241]|. The biosynthesis pathway of brassinolide was initially elucidated using cultured |FRAME:TAX-4058| cells. Extensive metabolic studies suggested that brassinosteroids biosynthesis occurs along a metabolic grid where broad substrate specificities of the participating enzymes allow metabolite flow via multiple pathways, depending on the order in which the enzymes act. Two such pathways, named the early and late C-6 oxidation pathways, were the first to be described |CITS: [9121728][10982435][14502988]| (see |FRAME:PWY-699|). A third route, named the early C-22 oxidation branch (depicted here) was also demonstrated |CITS:[12376657]|. The existence of this alternative biosynthesis branch has been postulated from the study of the &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; &lt;i&gt;sax1&lt;/i&gt; (|FRAME: AT5G05690|) and &lt;i&gt;det2&lt;/i&gt; (|FRAME: AT2G38050|) mutants. The &lt;i&gt;sax1&lt;/i&gt; phenotype could be rescued by application of synthetic |FRAME: CPD-3945| and |FRAME: CPD-3946|, compounds that were later detected &lt;i&gt;in planta&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[12376657][11489171]|. This complementation could also occur in the dark, suggesting that the C22&amp;alpha;-hydroxy pathway is active in dark conditions |CITS: [10377996]|. The &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: AT3G50660| gene, which encodes a |FRAME: AT3G50660-MONOMER|, was subsequently cloned |CITS: [11319239][11489171]|, as were the |FRAME: AT4G36380| and |FRAME: AT3G13730| genes, which encode redundantly functioning (22&lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;)-22-hydroxysteroid 23-monooxygenases |CITS: [17138693]|. &lt;i&gt;In vitro&lt;/i&gt; kinetic analysis of |FRAME: AT3G50660| showed that |FRAME: CPD-707| is its major substrate (the catalytic efficiency (Kcat/Km) for |FRAME: CPD-707| was 325 times higher than that for |FRAME: CPD-710| |CITS: [16460510]|), suggesting that the C-22 hydroxylation branch is the main route of brassinosteroid biosynthesis in plants (at least in Arabidopsis) |CITS: [16460510]|. This conclusion was supported by a later study that performed detailed kinetic analysis of |FRAME: AT5G05690| and |FRAME: AT2G38050| |CITS: [22822057]|. As of 2021, several reaction steps remain unelucidated: the enzymes forming |FRAME: CPD-698| from |FRAME: CPD-707|, the 3-dehydrogenase converting |FRAME: CPD-709| to |FRAME: CPD-710|, the C-6 oxidation step of |FRAME: CPD-710|, |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.M54|, and |FRAME: EC-1.1.99.M2| |CITS: [30363110]|.</Description>
